    "content": [
      {
        "id": "cgei-rs-45-3-cooling",
        "kind": "prose",
        "title": "Cooling intensity and distance",
        "body": "LST profiles away from parks or water can show cooling intensity (centre-to-surround difference) and a distance over which the anomaly decays. Patch-size thresholds are landscape- and climate-specific—not universal design codes. Report the index, season, time of day, and whether irrigation is present."
      },
      {
        "id": "cgei-rs-45-3-arid",
        "kind": "prose",
        "title": "Arid cities and irrigation",
        "body": "In arid and semi-arid cities, irrigated greenery may cool locally while consuming scarce water. Non-irrigated native cover behaves differently. Separate vegetation amount from configuration and irrigation status before recommending “more green” as cost-free cooling."
      },
      {
        "id": "cgei-rs-45-3-points",
        "kind": "key-points",
        "title": "Green/blue literacy",
        "items": [
          "Cooling metrics need stated profiles and seasons",
          "Irrigation confounds vegetation–LST links",
          "Configuration can matter beyond mean NDVI",
          "Use associational language unless design supports more"
        ]
      }
    ],
